# Week 6 — Conceptual Domain Modelling (UML Class Diagrams)


## Part A — Review of Earlier Work
### Task A1: Requirement Evidence Identification
1. Which requirement tells us that Booking is an important concept
   FR01 and FR02: a student can make more than one booking, and each booking identifies one equipment item.
2. Which requirement identifies two attributes of Booking?
   FR03: each booking records a start date and an end date
3. Which requirements indicate relationships involving Staff Member?
   FR04 staff review requests and FR05 staff manage equipment availability


## Part B — Identify Domain Concepts


### Task B1: Candidate Concept Table
Candidate | Class, Attribute, or Action? | Supporting Requirement 
Student | Class | FR01 
Booking | Class | FR01, FR02, FR03 
Start date | Attribute | FR03 
End date | Attribute | FR03 
Equipment | Class | FR01, FR02, FR05 
Review | Action | FR04 
Staff Member | Class | FR04, FR05 


### Task B2: Core Domain Classes Justification
Student: Represents the primary user who requests and holds equipment reservations within the system
Booking: Represents the central transactional domain record containing reservation dates, status, and item associations
Equipment: Represents the tangible resource being reserved and managed for availability
Staff Member: Represents the administrative role responsible for reviewing requests and overseeing equipment availability


### Task B3: Identification of Uncertainty
Candidate Attribute: emailAddress for Student or status for Booking
Uncertainty: While recording an email or booking status is logically useful, FR01–FR06 do not explicitly state how notifications or approval states are stored.
Equipment manager: College Facilities Manager / System Administrator


## Part E — Multiplicity & Open Questions

### Task E2 & E3: Multiplicity Analysis & Open Questions

#### OPEN QUESTIONS Note
1. Student — Booking: Can a booking be made jointly on behalf of multiple students, or is it strictly tied to exactly one student
2. Booking — Equipment: How many bookings can reference the same equipment item over time
3. Staff Member — Booking: Is each booking reviewed by exactly one assigned staff member, or can multiple staff members review the same request
4. Staff Member — Equipment: Can multiple staff members manage the same equipment item, or is equipment divided among specific staff


## Part F — Model Verification

### Task F1: Requirements-to-Model Check Table
Requirement | Where represented in diagram? | Fully represented? 
FR01 | Student — Booking association | Partially (Student side multiplicity requires confirmation) 
FR02 | Booking — Equipment association with 1 at Equipment end | Yes 
FR03 | startDate and endDate attributes in Booking class | Yes 
FR04 | Staff Member — Booking association (reviews) | Partially (Reviewer assignment rule unconfirmed) 
FR05 | Staff Member — Equipment association (manages availability of) | Yes 
FR06 | Candidate status attribute in Booking class | Needs clarification Approval workflow logic not detailed


## Part G — Campus Workshop Registration New Example
One Student can have zero or many Registration records
Each Registration belongs to exactly one Student
One Workshop can have zero or many Registration records
Each Registration is for exactly one Workshop


## Part H — Assessed Individual Project Application
Scenario A: Community Volunteer & Event Coordination System

### Task H1: Candidate Domain Concepts
Volunteer: a person who applies for event roles
Event: an activity organised by the community organisation
Role: a task in an event called EventRole in an earlier draft
Application: a volunteer's request to take a role
Event Organiser: a person who runs events

### Task H2 & H3: Associations and Multiplicities
Volunteer 1 — makes — 0..* Application. ASSUMED, to confirm.
Application 0..* — for — 1 Role. SUPPORTED a volunteer applies for a role at an event
Role 1..* — belongs to — 1 Event. SUPPORTED organisers create events and roles
Event 0..* — organised by — 1 Event Organiser. ASSUMED, to confirm. The scenario does not say who manages events.

### Notes about the diagram
The diagram has no names on the lines. The names above are the intended ones.

Unconfirmed attributes (not in the scenario, confirmation required):
Volunteer: vettingStatus, vettingExpiryDate, phoneNumber
Role: vettingRequired, capacity
Event: status
Application: status
Event Organiser: department

Garda vetting is not in the scenario. These vetting attributes are candidates only. The scenario says only that some activities need particular skills or experience (requiredSkills).

### Task H4: Open Question / Assumption
Question: Can a volunteer apply for several roles in the same event, or only one?
Target Stakeholder: Event Organiser
